## 3) Example stack config

## `Pulumi.dev.yaml`

```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  your-project:appName: webapp
  your-project:vpcId: vpc-xxxxxxxx
  your-project:privateSubnetIds:
    - subnet-aaaaaaa
    - subnet-bbbbbbb
  your-project:dbAllowedSecurityGroupIds:
    - sg-cccccccc
  your-project:oidcProviderArn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLE
  your-project:oidcIssuerUrl: https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLE
  your-project:k8sNamespace: app
  your-project:serviceAccountName: webapp
  your-project:dbName: appdb
  your-project:dbUsername: appuser
  your-project:dbPassword:
    secure: <encrypted-secret>
```

## `Pulumi.prod.yaml`

```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  your-project:appName: webapp
  your-project:vpcId: vpc-yyyyyyyy
  your-project:privateSubnetIds:
    - subnet-1111111
    - subnet-2222222
    - subnet-3333333
  your-project:dbAllowedSecurityGroupIds:
    - sg-dddddddd
  your-project:oidcProviderArn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/PROD
  your-project:oidcIssuerUrl: https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/PROD
  your-project:k8sNamespace: app
  your-project:serviceAccountName: webapp
  your-project:dbName: appdb
  your-project:dbUsername: appuser
  your-project:dbPassword:
    secure: <encrypted-secret>
```

Set the secret with:

```bash
pulumi config set --secret dbPassword 'super-secret-password'
```

---

## 4) Notes on dev/prod behavior

This implementation automatically adjusts the DB by stack:

- `dev`
  - `db.t3.micro`
  - single-AZ
  - 7-day retention

- `prod`
  - `db.t3.medium`
  - multi-AZ
  - 14-day retention
  - encrypted storage enabled

The bucket is configured in both environments with:

- versioning enabled
- server-side encryption enabled
- public access blocked

The IAM role is scoped to:

- one Kubernetes service account only
- only the required S3 bucket permissions
- no wildcard actions

